-10-19","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-21","committee":"Full Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-02","committee":"Families, Health and Care Policy and Accountability Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-03","committee":"Climate, Sustainability and Travel Policy and Accountability Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-04","committee":"Economy and Social Advancement Policy and Accountability Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-09","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-11","committee":"Policy and Oversight Board","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-22","committee":"Environment, Arts and Culture Policy and Accountability Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Pension Fund Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Housing and Homelessness Policy and Accountability Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Policy and Oversight Board","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Hammersmith and Fulham balanced accounts but faces \u00a331.4m budget gap in two years","lead":"Hammersmith and Fulham balanced its budget across all areas in 2025/26 despite financial pressures from inflation, interest rates and a cyber incident, the council's audit committee was told. However, the council faces substantial budget gaps when temporary government funding ends: \u00a331.4m in 2027/28 and \u00a357.3m in 2028/29, according to the council's auditors. Government changes to funding and business rates will cut local income by \u00a323.5m a year. The council holds \u00a3122.1m in reserves set aside, but auditors say these cannot sustainably cover recurring shortfalls and the council will need to consider Council Tax increases and additional savings.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Appendix 4 - LBHF Pension Fund Audit Findings Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://lbhf.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s136009/136009.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 7 - LBHF Pension Fund Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://lbhf.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s136011/136011.pdf","headline":"Fund identified medium-level risk that inadequate cash could force below-market asset sales to meet pension payments","detail":"The Fund's Risk Register identifies a medium-rated risk that if investment income declines, insufficient cash could necessitate selling investment assets at unfavorable prices to meet pension payment obligations. Mitigating actions include quarterly cash-flow forecasts and regular Committee reporting.","category":"other","matters":"Liquidity pressure could force the Fund to crystallise losses by selling assets during market downturns, damaging long-term returns and potentially creating pressure for higher council contributions to recover funding levels.","figures":"Risk rated Medium; Fund held \u00a317.9m cash deposits as at 31 March 2026","quote":"There is insufficient cash available to the Fund to meet pension payments due to reduced income generated from underlying investments, leading to investment assets being sold at sub-optimal prices to meet pension obligations.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 7 - LBHF Pension Fund Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://lbhf.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s136011/136011.pdf","headline":"New employer contribution rates established for 1 April 2026 based on completed 2025 actuarial valuation","detail":"The 2025 actuarial valuation was completed during the year and established new employer contribution requirements effective 1 April 2026. Current employer contribution rates for 2025/26 (from 2022 valuation) range from 13.5% to 35.8% depending on employer type. The strong 113% funding position typically supports stable or lower rates.","category":"governance","matters":"Employer contribution rates directly affect council budget planning and operating costs for all participating employers. The April 2026 rate-setting represents a material budgeting event for the council and schools.","figures":"Current rates: main council 21.7%, academy schools 13.5-23.0%, admitted bodies 20.3-35.8%; new rates effective 1 April 2026","quote":"The valuation confirmed the Fund's continued strong funding position and established employer contribution requirements from 1 April 2026.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 7 - LBHF Pension Fund Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://lbhf.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s136011/136011.pdf","headline":"Net cash outflow from pension benefits increased 51% to \u00a319.6 million, with payouts exceeding contributions","detail":"In 2025/26, pension and benefit payments totaled \u00a377.5 million against contributions of \u00a357.9 million, creating a net outflow of \u00a319.6 million. This outflow increased sharply from \u00a312.9 million in 2024/25, reflecting a maturing scheme structure requiring increasing reliance on investment income and asset sales to meet obligations.","category":"demand","matters":"A widening gap between pension payments and contribution income indicates structural cash-flow pressure that could force asset sales during market downturns, and means the council's contribution requirements could rise if investment returns disappoint.","figures":"Contributions \u00a357.9m; Total benefits \u00a377.5m; net outflow \u00a319.6m (2025/26); prior year outflow \u00a312.9m (51% increase)","quote":"Contributions (57,902); Pensions 77,491; Net (additions)/withdrawals from dealings with members 19,589","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 7 - LBHF Pension Fund Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://lbhf.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s136011/136011.pdf","headline":"Investment return of 3.5% significantly underperformed LGPS average by 5.43 percentage points","detail":"The Fund achieved 3.5% investment performance in 2025/26 (down from 3.7% in 2024/25), falling substantially below average LGPS fund returns. Though the Fund has outperformed its strategic benchmark over 10 years, this recent annual underperformance against peer funds represents a concerning trend.","category":"income","matters":"Sustained underperformance reduces investment returns available to fund liabilities and could force contribution rate increases in future valuations, creating budget pressure for the council and all employers.","figures":"3.5% return (2025/26); 3.7% return (2024/25); underperformance vs LGPS average 5.43 percentage points; 10-year outperformance vs benchmark 0.54%","quote":"In 2025/26, the Fund's investment performance was 3.5% (3.7% in 2024/25) to \u00a31.48bn. This was below the average LGPS return by -5.43%.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 7 - LBHF Pension Fund Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://lbhf.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s136011/136011.pdf","headline":"Actuarial funding level improved to 113% with \u00a3161 million surplus, up from 105% in 2022 valuation","detail":"The 2025 actuarial valuation confirmed the Fund's strong financial position with assets meeting 113% of accrued liabilities, generating a surplus of approximately \u00a3161 million. This represents a material improvement from the 105% funding level at the 2022 valuation, indicating strengthened solvency and reduced future contribution pressure.","category":"reserves","matters":"A stronger funding level reduces pressure on employer contribution increases and signals lower financial risk to creditors. It indicates the council's pension obligations are better-funded and more sustainable.","figures":"113% funding level (2025); \u00a3161 million surplus; 105% funding level (2022)","quote":"the valuation confirmed the Fund's strong financial position, with assets sufficient to meet 113% of accrued liabilities, representing a surplus of approximately \u00a3161 million and an improvement from the 105% funding level achieved at the 2022 valuation.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Hammersmith & Fulham","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-24","since_iso":"2026-08-25","distress":false,"headline":"Fund identified medium-level risk that inadequate cash could force below-market asset sales to meet pension payments","lead":"Liquidity pressure could force the Fund to crystallise losses by selling assets during market downturns, damaging long-term returns and potentially creating pressure for higher council contributions to recover funding levels.","also":["59 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit Committee on 2026-09-24","n_findings":60,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix 7 - LBHF Pension Fund Annual Report 2025/26","top_paper_url":"https://lbhf.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s136011/136011.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":126,"news_drafts_total":1}}